        "---\n",
        "jupyter: stats\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "# Logistic Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "1. probability estimation\n",
        "2. thresholding\n",
        "3. classification decisions\n",
        "4. evaluation metrics\n",
        "\n",
        "## Modeling Binary Outcomes\n",
        "\n",
        "In linear regression, we model a continuous response by\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p + \\varepsilon.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This framework is not appropriate when the response is binary, for example $Y \\in \\{0,1\\}$.  \n",
        "For a binary response, the main target is the conditional probability\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1 \\mid X=\\mathbf{x}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A logistic regression models this probability by\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y=1 \\mid X=\\mathbf{x}) = \\pi(\\mathbf{x})\n",
        "= \\frac{\\exp(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p)}\n",
        "{1+\\exp(\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently, if we write the linear predictor as\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\eta(\\mathbf{x}) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "then\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})}}{1+e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This guarantees that the fitted probability always stays between $0$ and $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Logistic regression is a GLM\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression is a special case of a generalized linear model.\n",
        "\n",
        "- The response distribution is Bernoulli (or Binomial).\n",
        "- The mean is the probability $\\pi(\\mathbf{x}) = P(Y=1\\mid X=\\mathbf{x})$.\n",
        "- The link function is the **logit** link.\n",
        "\n",
        "So the model is linear in the **log-odds**, not in the probability itself.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## The Logit Link and Odds\n",
        "\n",
        "Starting from\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})}}{1+e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})}},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "we obtain\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1-\\pi(\\mathbf{x}) = \\frac{1}{1+e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})}}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore the odds are\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\pi(\\mathbf{x})}{1-\\pi(\\mathbf{x})} = e^{\\eta(\\mathbf{x})},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "and taking logarithm gives\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi(\\mathbf{x})}{1-\\pi(\\mathbf{x})}\\right)\n",
        "= \\eta(\\mathbf{x})\n",
        "= \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence logistic regression can be written as\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{P(Y=1\\mid X=\\mathbf{x})}{P(Y=0\\mid X=\\mathbf{x})}\\right)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x_1 + \\cdots + \\beta_p x_p.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is why the coefficients are interpreted through **log-odds** and **odds ratios**.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Interpreting the Coefficients\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose all predictors except $x_j$ are fixed, and $x_j$ increases by one unit. Then the linear predictor increases by $\\beta_j$, so the log-odds increase by $\\beta_j$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Delta \\log(\\text{odds}) = \\beta_j.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Exponentiating both sides, we get\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{odds ratio} = e^{\\beta_j}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $\\beta_j$ is the change in **log-odds** associated with a one-unit increase in $x_j$.\n",
        "- $e^{\\beta_j}$ is the corresponding **odds ratio**.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Interpretation of a coefficient\n",
        "\n",
        "In logistic regression, the coefficient $\\beta_j$ does **not** represent the change in probability.\n",
        "\n",
        "Instead:\n",
        "\n",
        "- $\\beta_j$ is the change in **log-odds**\n",
        "- $e^{\\beta_j}$ is the multiplicative change in **odds**\n",
        "\n",
        "for a one-unit increase in $x_j$, with all other variables held fixed.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why Not Use Linear Regression?\n",
        "\n",
        "If we fit a linear regression model to a binary response, several problems arise.\n",
        "\n",
        "First, the fitted values are not restricted to the interval $[0,1]$, so they cannot always be interpreted as probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Second, the variance of a binary response depends on the mean:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\Var(Y\\mid X=\\mathbf{x}) = \\pi(\\mathbf{x})(1-\\pi(\\mathbf{x})),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "which violates the constant-variance assumption of ordinary linear regression.\n",
        "\n",
        "Third, the relationship between predictors and probability is often nonlinear, especially near probability $0$ or $1$. Logistic regression addresses these issues by modeling the log-odds linearly instead of the probability directly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prediction and Classification\n",
        "\n",
        "After fitting the model, we may compute the estimated probability\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat{\\pi}(\\mathbf{x}) = P(Y=1\\mid X=\\mathbf{x}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "If we want a class prediction, a common rule is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat{Y} =\n",
        "\\begin{cases}\n",
        "1, & \\hat{\\pi}(\\mathbf{x}) > 0.5,\\\\\n",
        "0, & \\hat{\\pi}(\\mathbf{x}) \\le 0.5.\n",
        "\\end{cases}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cutoff $0.5$ is common, but it is not mandatory. In practice, the threshold may be adjusted depending on the application and the relative cost of false positives and false negatives.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Estimation by Maximum Likelihood\n",
        "\n",
        "For binary data, each $Y_i$ follows a Bernoulli distribution with parameter $\\pi_i$:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "Y_i \\mid X_i \\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\pi_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(Y_i = y_i \\mid X_i)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\pi_i^{y_i}(1-\\pi_i)^{1-y_i},\n",
        "\\qquad y_i \\in \\{0,1\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For $n$ independent observations, the likelihood is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\pi_i^{y_i}(1-\\pi_i)^{1-y_i}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Taking logarithm, the log-likelihood is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        "y_i \\log(\\pi_i) + (1-y_i)\\log(1-\\pi_i)\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using the logistic form of $\\pi_i$, this becomes\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell(\\boldsymbol\\beta)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        "y_i \\mathbf{x}_i^\\top \\boldsymbol\\beta\n",
        "-\n",
        "\\log\\bigl(1+\\exp(\\mathbf{x}_i^\\top \\boldsymbol\\beta)\\bigr)\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Unlike OLS, logistic regression does not have a closed-form solution.  \n",
        "The coefficients are obtained numerically by maximizing the log-likelihood.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Deviance\n",
        "\n",
        "In generalized linear models, model fit is often summarized by the **deviance**. For logistic regression, a smaller deviance indicates a better fit.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two common quantities are\n",
        "\n",
        "- **Null deviance**: the deviance from a model with only an intercept\n",
        "- **Residual deviance**: the deviance from the fitted model\n",
        "\n",
        "A large reduction from null deviance to residual deviance suggests that the predictors provide useful information about the response.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## A Simple One-Variable Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the fitted model is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\log\\left(\\frac{\\pi(x)}{1-\\pi(x)}\\right)\n",
        "=\n",
        "-3.006 + 0.052x.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a subject with $x=55$, the linear predictor is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "-3.006 + 0.052(55) = -0.146.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Hence the estimated probability is\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi(55)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{e^{-0.146}}{1+e^{-0.146}}\n",
        "\\approx 0.463.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So the model predicts a probability of about $46.3\\%$ for class $1$.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python Implementation with `statsmodels`\n",
        "\n",
        "For classical logistic regression, `statsmodels` is often the most natural Python choice."
